## TL;DR
1-2 plain sentences: what the paper does and the single most important takeaway. No markdown formatting inside — this text is shown as-is in my notification feed.

## Problem
What problem this paper solves.

## Key Contributions
3-5 contributions (verifiable if possible).

## Teaser Figure
What the teaser (usually Figure 1) shows and the message it is designed to deliver, judged from its caption and how the text references it: what is being compared or illustrated, and why the authors chose THIS as the first thing a reader sees. If no teaser figure is identifiable in the text, write "Not mentioned."

## Intro Narrative
How the introduction builds its story, as a 3-6 step arc (e.g. "broad trend → concrete failure of prior work → why existing fixes fall short → key observation → contributions"). Capture the actual rhetorical moves of THIS paper, not a generic template: what it opens with, how it frames the gap, where the key insight lands.

## Method Summary
100-200 words explaining the technical approach.

## Method Writing
How the method section is written, not what it contains: its structure (problem formulation / preliminaries / subsection layout), the presentation style (notation-heavy vs figure-driven, algorithm boxes, running examples), and 1-2 concrete moves in the exposition worth borrowing for my own writing.

## Training Data
Data/synthetic data used.

## Benchmarks
Benchmarks/tasks used for evaluation.

## Baselines
Main comparisons and baselines.

## Claims
Claimed improvements (speed/performance/generalization/cost).

## Ablation
Whether ablation studies are included.

